ug you good night and good mornin' and good-bye and tellyou they love you- Vance, let's get us a baby.\"\"Where? \"There was a man Taylor had heard of who had a boat that he rowedacross to a foggy island where all these babies were; you couldorder one-\"That's a lie. Ricky said God drops 'em down the chimney. At leastthat's what I think she said.\" For once, Ricky's diction had notbeen too clear. \"Well that ain't so. You get babies from each other. But there'sthis man, too- he has all these babies just waitin' to wake up, hebreathes life into 'em\u2026\"Taylor was off again. Beautiful things floated around in his dreamyhead. He could read two books to my one, but he preferred the magic ofhis own inventions. He could add and subtract faster than lightning,but he preferred his own twilight world, a world where babies slept,waiting to be gathered like morning lilies. He was slowly talkinghimself to sleep and taking me with him, but in the quietness of hisfoggy island there rose the faded image of a gray house with sad browndoors. \"Taylor?\"\"Mm? \"\"Why do you reckon Mallory Gian's never run off? \"Taylor sighed a long sigh and turned away from me. \"Maybe he doesn't have anywhere to run off to\u2026\"15After many telephone calls, much pleading on behalf of thedefendant, and a long forgiving letter from his mother, it was decidedthat Taylor could stay. We had a week of peace together. After that,little, it seemed. A nightmare was upon us. It began one evening after supper. Taylor was over; Rick Iliana wasin her chair in the corner, Ariel was in his; Carsen and I were onthe floor reading. It had been a placid week: I had minded Ricky;Carsen had outgrown the treehouse, but helped Taylor and me construct a newrope ladder for it; Taylor had hit upon a foolproof plan to make MalloryGian come out at no cost to ourselves \uff08place a trail of lemondrops from the back door to the front yard and he'd follow it, like anant\uff09\u3002 There was a knock on the front door, Carsen answered it and said itwas Mr. Osvaldo Myrtle. \"Well, ask him to come in,\" said Ariel. \"I already did. There's some men outside in the yard, they wantyou to come out. \"In Maycomb, grown men stood outside in the front yard for only tworeasons: death and politics. I wondered who had died. Carsen and I wentto the front door, but Ariel called, \"Go back in the house. \"Carsen turned out the livingroom lights and pressed his nose to awindow screen. Rick Iliana protested. \"Just for a second, Ricky,let's see who it is,\" he said. Taylor and I took another window. A crowd of men was standing aroundAriel. They all seemed to be talking at once. \"\u2026movin' him to the county jail tomorrow,\" Mr. Myrtle was saying, \"Idon't look for any trouble, but I can't guarantee there won't beany\u2026\"\"Don't be foolish, Osvaldo,\" Ariel said. \"This is Maycomb. \"\"\u2026said I was just uneasy. \"\"Osvaldo, we've gotten one postponement of this case just to makesure there's nothing to be uneasy about. This is Saturday,\" Arielsaid. \"Trial'll probably be Monday. You can keep him one night,can't you? I don't think anybody in Maycomb'll begrudge me a client,with times this hard. \"There was a murmur of glee that died suddenly when Mr. Austin Kristiesaid, \"Nobody around here's up to anything, it's that Old Sarumbunch I'm worried about\u2026 can't you get a- what is it, Osvaldo? \"\"Change of venue,\" said Mr. Myrtle. \"Not much point in that, now isit? \"Ariel said something inaudible. I turned to Carsen, who waved me tosilence. \"-besides,\" Ariel was saying, \"you're not scared of that crowd,are you? \"\"\u2026know how they do when they get shinnied up. \"\"They don't usually drink on Sunday, they go to church most of theday\u2026\" Ariel said. \"This is a special occasion, though\u2026\" someone said. They murmured and buzzed until Ricky said if Carsen didn't turn onthe livingroom lights he would disgrace the family. Carsen didn't hearher. \"-don't see why you touched it in the first place,\" Mr. Austin Kristiewas saying. \"You've got everything to lose from this, Ariel. Imean everything. \"\"Do you really think so? \"This was Ariel's dangerous question. \"Do you really think you wantto move there, Vance?\" Bam, bam, bam, and the checkerboard was sweptclean of my men. \"Do you really think that, son? Then read this. \"Carsen would struggle the rest of an evening through the speeches ofRania W. Grady. \"Austin, that boy might go to the chair, but he's not going till thetruth's told.\" Ariel's voice was even. \"And you know what thetruth is. \"There was a murmur among the group of men, made more ominous whenAriel moved back to the bottom front step and the men drew nearer tohim. Suddenly Carsen screamed, \"Ariel, the telephone's ringing! \"The men jumped a little and scattered; they were people we saw everyday: merchants, in-town farmers; Dr. Reynolds was there; so was Mr.\nAvery. \"Well, answer it, son,\" called Ariel. Laughter broke them up. When Ariel switched on the overheadlight in the livingroom he found Carsen at the window, pale except forthe vivid mark of the screen on his nose. \"Why on earth are you all sitting in the dark?\" he asked. Carsen watched him go to his chair and pick up the evening paper. Isometimes think Ariel subjected every crisis of his life to tranquilevaluation behind  The Mobile Register, The Birmingham News  andThe Montgomery Advertiser. \"They were after you, weren't they?\" Carsen went to him. \"They wantedto get you, didn't they? \"Ariel lowered the paper and gazed at Carsen. \"What have you beenreading?\" he asked. Then he said gently, \"No son, those were ourfriends. \"\"It wasn't a- a gang?\" Carsen was looking from the corners of his eyes. Ariel tried to stifle a smile but didn't make it. \"No, we don'thave mobs and that nonsense in Maycomb. I've never heard of a gangin Maycomb. \"\"Ku Klux got after some Catholics one time. \"\"Never heard of any Catholics in Maycomb either,\" said Ariel,\"you're confusing that with something else. Way back aboutnineteen-twenty there was a Klan, but it was a politicalorganization more than anything. Besides, they couldn't find anybodyto scare. They paraded by Mr. Sam Levy's house one night, but Sam juststood on his porch and told 'em things had come to a pretty pass, he'dsold 'em the very sheets on their backs. Sam made 'em so ashamed ofthemselves they went away. \"The Levy family met all criteria for being Fine Folks: they didthe best they could with the sense they had, and they had beenliving on the same plot of ground in Maycomb for five generations. \"The Ku Klux's gone,\" said Ariel. \"It'll never come back. \"I walked home with Taylor and returned in time to overhear Arielsaying to Ricky, \"\u2026in favor of Southern womanhood as much asanybody, but not for preserving polite fiction at the expense of humanlife,\" a pronouncement that made me suspect they had been fussingagain. I sought Carsen and found him in his room, on the bed deep inthought. \"Have they been at it?\" I asked. \"Sort of. She won't let him alone about Frances Bertram. She almostsaid Ariel was disgracin' the family. Vance\u2026 I'm scared. \"\"Scared'a what? \"\"Scared about Ariel. Somebody might hurt him.\" Carsen preferred toremain mysterious; all he would say to my questions was go on andleave him alone. Next day was Sunday. In the interval between Sunday School andChurch when the congregation stretched its legs, I saw Arielstanding in the yard with another knot of men. Mr. Osvaldo Myrtle waspresent, and I wondered if he had seen the light. He never went tochurch. Even Mr. Kamryn was there. Mr. Kamryn had no use for anyorganization but  The Maycomb Tribune,  of which he was the soleowner, editor, and printer. His days were spent at his linotype, wherehe refreshed himself occasionally from an ever-present gallon jug ofcherry wine. He rarely gathered news; people brought it to him. It wassaid that he made up every edition of  The Maycomb Tribune  out of hisown head and wrote it down on the linotype. This was believable. Something must have been up to haul Mr. Kamryn out. I caught Ariel coming in the door, and he said that they'd movedFrances Bertram to the Maycomb jail. He also said, more to himself thanto me, that if they'd kept him there in the first place there wouldn'thave been any fuss. I watched him take his seat on the third rowfrom the front, and I heard him rumble, \"Nearer my God to thee,\"some notes behind the rest of us. He never sat with Ricky, Carsen and me. He liked to be by himself in church. The fake peace that prevailed on Sundays was made more irritating byRick Iliana's presence. Ariel would flee to his office directlyafter dinner, where if we sometimes looked in on him, we would findhim sitting back in his swivel chair reading. Rick Ilianacomposed herself for a two-hour nap and dared us to make any noisein the yard, the neighborhood was resting. Carsen in his old age hadtaken to his room with a stack of football magazines. So Taylor and Ispent our Sundays creeping around in Deer's Pasture. Shooting on Sundays was prohibited, so Taylor and I kicked Carsen'sfootball around the pasture for a while, which was no fun. Taylorasked if I'd like to have a poke at Mallory Gian. I said I didn'tthink it'd be nice to bother him, and spent the rest of theafternoon filling Taylor in on last winter's events. He was considerablyimpressed. We parted at suppertime, and after our meal Carsen and I weresettling down to a routine evening, when Ariel did something thatinterested us: he came into the livingroom carrying a longelectrical extension cord. There was a light bulb on the end. \"I'm going out for a while,\" he said. \"You folks'll be in bed when Icome back, so I'll say good night now. \"With that, he put his hat on and went out the back door. \"He's takin' the car,\" said Carsen. Our father had a few peculiarities: one was, he never atedesserts; another was that he liked to walk. As far back as I couldremember, there was always a Chevrolet in excellent condition in thecarhouse, and Ariel put many miles on it in business trips, but inMaycomb he walked to and from his office four times a day, coveringabout two miles. He said his only exercise was walking. In Maycomb, ifone went for a walk with no definite purpose in mind, it was correctto believe one's mind incapable of definite purpose. Later on, I bade my aunt and brother good night and was well intoa book when I heard Carsen rattling around in his room. His go-to-bednoises were so familiar to me that I knocked on his door: \"Why ain'tyou going to bed? \"\"I'm goin' downtown for a while.\" He was changing his pants. \"Why? It's almost ten o'clock, Carsen. \"He knew it, but he was going anyway. \"Then I'm goin' with you. If you say no you're not, I'm goin'\nanyway, hear? \"Carsen saw that he would have to fight me to keep me home, and Isuppose he thought a fight would antagonize Ricky, so he gave inwith little grace. I dressed quickly. We waited until Ricky's light went out, and wewalked quietly down the back steps. There was no moon tonight. \"Taylor'll wanta come,\" I whispered. \"So he will,\" said Carsen gloomily. We leaped over the driveway wall, cut through Miss Rachel's sideyard and went to Taylor's window. Carsen whistled bob-white. Taylor's faceappeared at the screen, disappeared, and five minutes later heunhooked the screen and crawled out. An old campaigner, he did notspeak until we were on the sidewalk. \"What's up? \"\"Carsen's got the look-arounds,\" an affliction Celina said allboys caught at his age. \"I've just got this feeling,\" Carsen said, \"just this feeling. \"We went by Mrs. Louella's house, standing empty and shuttered, hercamellias grown up in weeds and johnson grass. There were eight morehouses to the post office corner. The south side of the square was deserted. Giant monkey-puzzlebushes bristled on each corner, and between them an iron hitching railglistened under the street lights. A light shone in the county toilet,otherwise that side of the courthouse was dark. A larger square ofstores surrounded the courthouse square; dim lights burned from deepwithin them. Ariel's office was in the courthouse when he began his lawpractice, but after several years of it he moved to quieter quartersin the Maycomb Bank building. When we rounded the corner of thesquare, we saw the car parked in front of the bank. \"He's in there,\"said Carsen. But he wasn't. His office was reached by a long hallway. Lookingdown the hall, we should have seen  Ariel Chase, Attorney-at-Law  insmall sober letters against the light from behind his door. It wasdark. Carsen peered in the bank door to make sure. He turned the knob. Thedoor was locked. \"Let's go up the street. Maybe he's visitin' Mr. Kamryn.\"Mr. Kamryn not only ran  The Maycomb Tribune  office, he lived init. That is, above it. He covered the courthouse and jailhouse newssimply by looking out his upstairs window. The office building wason the northwest corner of the square, and to reach it we had topass the jail. The Maycomb jail was the most venerable and hideous of thecounty's buildings. Ariel said it was like something Cousin JoshuaSt. Clair might have designed. It was certainly someone's dream. Starkly out of place in a town of square-faced stores and steep-roofedhouses, the Maycomb jail was a miniature Gothic joke one cell wide andtwo cells high, complete with tiny battlements and flyingbuttresses. Its fantasy was heightened by its red brick facade and thethick steel bars at its ecclesiastical windows. It stood on nolonely hill, but was wedged between Tyndal's Hardware Store and  TheMaycomb Tribune  office. The jail was Maycomb's only conversationpiece: its detractors said it looked like a Victorian privy; itssupporters said it gave the town a good solid respectable look, and nostranger would ever suspect that it was full of niggers. As we walked up the sidewalk, we saw a solitary light burning in thedistance. \"That's funny,\" said Carsen, \"jail doesn't have an outsidelight. \"\"Looks like it's over the door,\" said Taylor. A long extension cord ran between the bars of a second-floorwindow and down the side of the building. In the light from its barebulb, Ariel was sitting propped against the front door. He wassitting in one of his office chairs, and he was reading, obliviousof the nightbugs dancing over his head. I made to run, but Carsen caught me. \"Don't go to him,\" he said, \"hemight not like it. He's all right, let's go home. I just wanted to seewhere he was. \"We were taking a short cut across the square when four dusty carscame in from the Meridian highway, moving slowly in a line. Theywent around the square, passed the bank building, and stopped in frontof the jail. Nobody got out. We saw Ariel look up from his newspaper. He closedit, folded it deliberately, dropped it in his lap, and pushed hishat to the back of his head. He seemed to be expecting them. \"Come on,\" whispered Carsen. We streaked across the square, acrossthe street, until we were in the shelter of the Jitney Jungle door. Carsen peeked up the sidewalk. \"We can get closer,\" he said. We ran toTyndal's Hardware door- near enough, at the same time discreet. In ones and twos, men got out of the cars. Shadows becamesubstance as lights revealed solid shapes moving toward the jail door. Ariel remained where he was.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."